# What NOT to send to the Docker daemon when building.
#
# Everything in the build context is uploaded to the daemon before the first
# instruction runs, so this file is the difference between a build that starts
# instantly and one that spends thirty seconds shipping a virtualenv it is about
# to throw away. `.venv/` alone is several hundred megabytes here.
#
# It is also a *security* file, not just a speed one. `COPY . .` in some future
# edit of the Dockerfile would otherwise bake `.env` — real database
# credentials and the JWT signing key — into an image layer, where it survives
# even if a later layer deletes the file, and travels to wherever that image is
# pushed. Excluding it here means that mistake cannot be made.

# ---- Virtual environments (the big one) ----
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/

# ---- Secrets ----
# Never in an image. Configuration reaches the container through environment
# variables at *run* time (see docker-compose.yml), which is the whole reason
# app/core/config.py reads its settings from the environment.
.env
*.env
!.env.example

# ---- Git ----
# The full history, including any secret ever committed and later removed.
.git/
.gitattributes
.gitignore

# ---- Python noise ----
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/

# ---- Tooling caches and test artifacts ----
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# ---- Not needed at runtime ----
# The tests run against the app, they are not part of it. Excluding them keeps
# the image smaller and means a container has no test fixtures lying around.
tests/
docs/
README.md
Dockerfile
docker-compose.yml
.dockerignore

# ---- IDE / OS ----
.idea/
.vscode/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.log
